ASSERT ok   R4.17 CONFORMS under revision 2: an explicit expose list reaches the browser client as access-control-expose-headers naming each standard-bound header
ASSERT ok   R4.17 was VIOLATED by revision 1: with no expose list configured the response carries no access-control-expose-headers at all, so a cross-origin browser client could not read request-id — this measurement is why the recommendation moved
ASSERT ok   and the wildcard form emits a bare '*', which lists no header name, so it does not satisfy R4.17's instruction to LIST the headers emitted
ASSERT ok   R4.17 is not engaged on a preflight response: it carries none of the standard-bound headers, and tower-http correspondingly omits access-control-expose-headers there
ASSERT ok   R5.11 CONFORMS: with the allowlist empty the layer is absent and OPTIONS on a GET-only route answers 405 carrying Allow, which is the header R5.11 makes mandatory
ASSERT ok   R11.7 is VIOLATED when the request-id layer sits inside CorsLayer: the preflight short-circuits and the 200 carries no request-id
ASSERT ok   R11.7 CONFORMS when it sits outside: the same preflight then carries request-id — the ordering constraint this item hands to R75, now required by a rule rather than only by py's precedent
ASSERT ok   R4.11 CONFORMS with the allowlist empty: no layer, no negotiation, and correspondingly no Vary, which is R4.11's own stated exception
ASSERT ok   R4.11 CONFORMS with the allowlist configured: Origin influenced the selection of access-control-allow-origin, which is withheld here for a disallowed origin, and Vary names it
ASSERT ok   R4.10 is not engaged by the preflight: that 200 carries content-length 0 and no content-type, so no representation was selected and no media-type negotiation arises

